  (:adr/body "2026-09-01. The custody question for an autonomous buyer, answered by what a
passkey can and cannot do, and by two things measured on Base rather than
recalled.

## A passkey cannot sign a payment, and that is the design

WebAuthn assertions require a user gesture. An agent producing one on its own
is a contradiction, and the ways around it -- user verification discouraged, a
server-side authenticator -- discard the hardware custody that made the passkey
worth choosing.

So the passkey never signs a payment. It signs a GRANT, rarely, with a person
present. The agent holds a session key and signs payments with that, often,
under a bound the grant set. This is the shape the biscuit work already has: a
root that issues and a holder that can only narrow. The passkey is how the root
key stops being a thing anyone can steal, because it stops being a thing anyone
can extract.

## One declaration, two enforcements

A spend limit ends up enforced in two places that cannot see each other: a
biscuit off-chain, a contract on-chain. Written separately they drift, and a
drifted pair has no honest reading -- the smaller one holds and nobody knows
which that is.

So both derive from manifest/spend-grants.edn. scripts/spend-grant-facts.cljs
is the single conversion, dollars to base units, and no other file may hold a
base-unit number: USDC has six decimals and a cap in the wrong unit is wrong by
a factor of a million in whichever direction hurts.

Verified end to end on 2026-09-01: the declared 0.05 dollars becomes 50000 base
units, folds into the policy pay.x402-buyer plans with, pays a live 0.001
challenge, and refuses a 0.50 offer as over-cap.

## The first limit needs no code

The account balance. An account holding five dollars cannot spend six, and
arithmetic enforces that with no contract, no audit and no review. The
per-call cap in the token is smaller and exists for a different reason: the
balance bounds the blast radius over time, the token bounds it per request.

A contract earns its place when the limit must be finer than how much is in the
account -- per day, per seller, per period. Until then, deploying one adds the
only irreversible thing in this design, to hold funds it does not yet need to
hold. This workspace has had six occasions in two days where something believed
absent turned out to exist; that is not the week to ship an unreviewed contract
that holds money.

## When the contract does come, it is a direct call

Two measurements decide this, and both were run rather than remembered.

The P-256 precompile at 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000100 works on
Base mainnet. A signature produced by this workspace's own org-nist-ecc, packed
into the RIP-7212 shape, returns one. So a passkey assertion can be verified
on-chain without a hand-written Solidity verifier and its hundreds of thousands
of gas.

The x402 rail identifies a payer by the ERC-20 Transfer log topics, not by
transaction.from. So a smart account paying USDC is recognised as the payer no
matter who submitted the transaction, and a relayer paying gas does not corrupt
the identity. ERC-4337 exists for gas sponsorship, and there is nobody to
sponsor yet; a direct call needs no bundler and composes with the transaction
scheme the rail already runs.

## What was done, and what deliberately was not

Done: the session key for dougaka exists, generated straight into the macOS
keychain with no copy on disk, and its address is in the declaration. The
declaration records where the key lives and never the key.

Not done: funding it. The address holds zero. Moving money is the operator step
and an agent should not take it, so the grant rests at awaiting-funding, which
is the correct state for a declaration whose signer does not exist yet -- the
passkey root is step two and is not built.

Also not done: the host seam that signs and retries. It stays unwritten until
there is a funded buyer to call it, because a seam with no caller is not
progress.")))